<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tiberius Gracchus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 163–<a href="/years/133-bce">133 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a> Gracchus, in full Tiberius Sempronius Gracchus, was a Roman tribune of the plebs in 133 BCE. He passed a law to redistribute public land to poor citizens over the Senate's opposition and then sought a second term. A band of senators led by Scipio Nasica clubbed him to death, the first open political killing in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> for centuries, which began a century of political violence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tiberius-gracchus.jpg" alt="Tiberius Gracchus" width="320" height="443" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jean-Baptiste Claude Eugène Guillaume · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eugene_Guillaume_-_the_Gracchi_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Roman Senate resisted land reform, and in 133 BCE senators clubbed to death the tribune <b>Tiberius Gracchus</b>; a century of political violence and civil war followed until the Republic fell.</blockquote><a href="/government/aristocracy">Aristocracy · Weaknesses</a></li><li><blockquote>The tribune <b>Tiberius Gracchus</b> was killed in 133 BCE, and in 121 BCE the Senate passed its first “final decree” against his brother Gaius Gracchus; Sulla marched on Rome and made himself dictator, Julius Caesar crossed into Italy with his army in 49 BCE, and after his murder…</blockquote><a href="/government/republic">Republic · Through History</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=-144" aria-label="Rome on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4854 672 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Tiberius Gracchus"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5304 942h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=-144">What was here in 144 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;They fought indeed, and were slain, but it was to maintain the luxury and the wealth of other men. They were styled the masters of the world, but in the meantime had not one foot of ground which they could call their own.&#8221;</p><footer>Plutarch, Life of Tiberius Gracchus &#183; tr. John Dryden and others, rev. A. H. Clough, 1859 &#183; Plutarch's account of his speech for the land law, 133 BCE, on the Roman soldiers &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/674/pg674-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 539 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
